course: dictionary
lesson: 3306
title: Ethylene: the gas that ripens fruit
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ethylene is a gas, so you cannot see it or smell it, and it has no colour. «Ripening is controlled by a gas called ethylene that fruit gives off.» Fruit makes this gas itself, and the gas tells the fruit to ripen.
explain: «Tomatoes ripen faster next to bananas because bananas give off a gas called ethylene.» Ethylene spreads from one fruit to the fruit around it. That is why one rotten apple can spoil the whole bowl, and why a hard green banana ripens fast in a paper bag with an apple.
explain: Ethylene is also a useful chemical. Factories use it to make plastics such as polythene. In school chemistry, scientists call it ethene, so if you see either word, they mean the same gas.
fact: Ethylene is a colourless gas made of carbon and hydrogen.
fact: Plants make ethylene naturally, and it makes fruit ripen.
fact: «Ethylene» is an uncountable noun, so you say some ethylene, not two ethylenes.
fact: Factories use ethylene to make plastics such as polythene (polyethylene).
fact: In school chemistry, ethylene is also called ethene.
exercise: «Ripening is controlled by a gas called ethylene that fruit gives off.» What is ethylene? | A type of plastic bag | A kind of sugar | A coloured liquid that fruit squeezes out | *A colourless gas that makes fruit ripen
exercise: «A ripe apple gives off a gas called ethylene that tells nearby fruit to ripen.» What does ethylene do to nearby fruit? | Turns it into stone | Makes it invisible | *Makes it ripen | Makes it grow hairs
exercise: Which two words name the same gas in school chemistry? | ethene and fruit | ethylene and polythene | *ethylene and ethene | banana and apple
test: «Tomatoes ripen faster next to bananas because bananas give off a gas called ethylene.» Why do tomatoes ripen faster? | They are being shaken | *Ethylene from the bananas reaches them | They are made of plastic | They are in the sun
test: Which use of ethylene happens in factories? | Growing crops in fields | Making bread | *Making plastics such as polythene | Cooling ice creams
exam: Why do shippers keep bananas cold and away from ripe fruit on long trips? | *So the ethylene from ripe fruit does not ripen the bananas too soon | So the bananas stay hot and soft | So the bananas can be turned into plastic | They want the bananas to get bigger
